1
00:00:00,020 --> 00:00:03,210
Every summer, our cities
seem to get a little hotter.

2
00:00:03,520 --> 00:00:08,520
In this talk, we will look at why that
happens and at the simple ideas that can

3
00:00:08,520 --> 00:00:10,410
help a city stay cool.

4
00:00:10,800 --> 00:00:12,550
Let us start with a question.

5
00:00:12,880 --> 00:00:16,570
Why is the middle of a city often
warmer than the countryside around it?

6
00:00:16,800 --> 00:00:19,720
Scientists call this the
urban heat island effect.

7
00:00:20,060 --> 00:00:23,680
During the day, roads, roofs,
and walls soak up sunlight.

8
00:00:23,960 --> 00:00:28,550
Dark surfaces, like asphalt, absorb
most of the energy that falls on them.

9
00:00:28,740 --> 00:00:32,310
At night, they slowly release
that heat back into the air.

10
00:00:32,520 --> 00:00:37,640
In a field or a forest, the ground stays
cooler because plants shade the soil and

11
00:00:37,640 --> 00:00:39,610
release water into the air.

12
00:00:39,980 --> 00:00:45,320
So after sunset, the countryside cools
down quickly, while the city stays warm

13
00:00:45,320 --> 00:00:46,320
for hours.

14
00:00:46,740 --> 00:00:48,120
There is more.

15
00:00:48,340 --> 00:00:52,100
Cars, air conditioners, and
factories all release heat.

16
00:00:52,340 --> 00:00:56,920
Tall buildings block the wind, so warm
air gets trapped in narrow streets.

17
00:00:56,920 --> 00:01:01,620
And when there are few trees, there is
little shade for people walking or waiting

18
00:01:01,620 --> 00:01:02,840
at a bus stop.

19
00:01:03,500 --> 00:01:04,800
Why does this matter?

20
00:01:04,980 --> 00:01:06,700
Heat is not only uncomfortable.

21
00:01:07,100 --> 00:01:10,220
On very hot days, more
people need medical care,

22
00:01:10,380 --> 00:01:14,750
especially older people, small
children, and those who work outside.

23
00:01:15,100 --> 00:01:19,350
Hot nights make it hard to sleep,
and tired people are less productive.

24
00:01:19,720 --> 00:01:24,380
Cities also use more electricity for
cooling, which costs money and can put a

25
00:01:24,380 --> 00:01:26,010
strain on the power grid.

26
00:01:26,320 --> 00:01:30,380
The good news is that we already
know many ways to cool a city down.

27
00:01:30,640 --> 00:01:33,500
The first and simplest
idea is to plant trees.

28
00:01:33,860 --> 00:01:36,570
A large tree can shade a
whole section of a street.

29
00:01:36,840 --> 00:01:41,240
Its leaves release water vapor, which
cools the air around it, a little like

30
00:01:41,240 --> 00:01:42,760
sweat cools our skin.

31
00:01:43,080 --> 00:01:47,540
Streets lined with trees can be several
degrees cooler than bare streets nearby.

32
00:01:47,980 --> 00:01:50,180
Parks work the same way on a bigger scale.

33
00:01:50,180 --> 00:01:55,020
Even small pocket parks built on empty
corners can make a difference to the

34
00:01:55,020 --> 00:01:56,640
people who live next to them.

35
00:01:56,900 --> 00:02:00,520
The second idea is to change
the color of our surfaces.

36
00:02:00,900 --> 00:02:05,160
A white or light colored roof reflects
much of the sunlight back into the sky

37
00:02:05,160 --> 00:02:07,320
instead of turning it into heat.

38
00:02:07,580 --> 00:02:11,930
In some cities, people paint their
roofs with special reflective paint.

39
00:02:12,220 --> 00:02:17,400
The rooms below stay cooler, and the
family uses less power for fans and air

40
00:02:17,400 --> 00:02:17,800
conditioning.

41
00:02:17,800 --> 00:02:23,180
The same idea can be used for pavements,
playgrounds and parking areas.

42
00:02:23,760 --> 00:02:27,140
The third idea is green
roofs and green walls.

43
00:02:27,500 --> 00:02:30,440
A green roof is covered
with soil and plants.

44
00:02:30,780 --> 00:02:35,820
It keeps the building below cooler, holds
rainwater after a storm and gives birds

45
00:02:35,820 --> 00:02:37,740
and insects a place to live.

46
00:02:38,060 --> 00:02:43,350
Green walls, covered with climbing plants,
do the same for the sides of buildings.

47
00:02:43,720 --> 00:02:46,040
The fourth idea is water.

48
00:02:46,420 --> 00:02:50,810
Fountains, ponds, canals and
lakes all help to cool the air.

49
00:02:51,100 --> 00:02:55,520
Many old cities were built around water
tanks and step wells for exactly this

50
00:02:55,520 --> 00:02:56,230
reason.

51
00:02:56,520 --> 00:03:01,460
Today, some cities are bringing back
buried streams and turning them into green

52
00:03:01,460 --> 00:03:04,080
paths where people can walk and cycle.

53
00:03:04,640 --> 00:03:07,120
The fifth idea is smarter building design.

54
00:03:07,120 --> 00:03:11,060
Traditional houses in hot
regions often had thick walls,

55
00:03:11,340 --> 00:03:15,130
small windows, shaded
courtyards and high ceilings.

56
00:03:15,420 --> 00:03:18,730
These features kept the inside
cool without any machines.

57
00:03:19,120 --> 00:03:22,140
Modern architects are learning
from these designs again.

58
00:03:22,420 --> 00:03:27,760
Windows that face away from the afternoon
sun, wide balconies that cast shade,

59
00:03:28,020 --> 00:03:32,520
and gaps that let the wind flow through a
building can all reduce the need for air

60
00:03:32,520 --> 00:03:33,440
conditioning.

61
00:03:33,920 --> 00:03:37,910
Finally, cities can plan for heat
the way they plan for floods.

62
00:03:38,260 --> 00:03:41,470
Some cities open cooling
centers on the hottest days,

63
00:03:41,720 --> 00:03:46,360
such as libraries and community halls
where anyone can rest in the shade and

64
00:03:46,360 --> 00:03:47,780
drink clean water.

65
00:03:48,180 --> 00:03:51,680
Others send text messages to
warn people before a heat wave

66
00:03:51,680 --> 00:03:55,920
arrives and change working hours
for people who work outdoors.

67
00:03:56,620 --> 00:03:58,120
People can help at home too.

68
00:03:58,120 --> 00:04:02,760
Closing curtains on the sunny side of the
house during the afternoon keeps rooms

69
00:04:02,760 --> 00:04:03,570
cooler.

70
00:04:03,900 --> 00:04:08,450
A few pots of plants on a balcony give
shade and cool the air near a window.

71
00:04:08,800 --> 00:04:13,660
Opening windows on two sides of a room at
night lets the cool breeze flow through

72
00:04:13,660 --> 00:04:18,620
and checking on an elderly neighbor
during a heat wave can save a life.

73
00:04:19,020 --> 00:04:22,350
None of these ideas is
very expensive on its own.

74
00:04:22,620 --> 00:04:27,960
A tree, a can of white paint or a
shaded bus stop may look small but when

75
00:04:27,960 --> 00:04:32,480
thousands of people in many neighborhoods
do these things together the whole city

76
00:04:32,480 --> 00:04:33,720
becomes cooler,

77
00:04:33,960 --> 00:04:36,210
healthier and more pleasant to live in.

78
00:04:36,480 --> 00:04:39,910
So the next time you walk
down a hot street look around.

79
00:04:40,180 --> 00:04:45,220
Notice where the shade is, where the dark
surfaces are and where a tree or a light

80
00:04:45,220 --> 00:04:46,820
colored roof could help.

81
00:04:47,100 --> 00:04:51,520
A cooler city starts with small
choices made one street at a time.

82
00:04:51,760 --> 00:04:53,180
Thank you for listening.

